  "summary_md": "The furnishings sector is navigating soft consumer demand and tariff uncertainty, but the batch reveals sharp divergences. SNBR’s Chapter 11 filing confirms an existential restructuring after a product reset failed, while AMWD’s pending MasterBrand acquisition creates a defining M&A overhang. In contrast, EFOI’s pivot into energy storage and a data-center contract triggered a sharp re-rating, and operational discipline delivered margin gains at HOFT, LCUT, HBB, and FLXS despite weak top-lines. Tariff maneuvering (FGI, HBB) and dividend sustainability doubts (CRWS) are recurring themes.\n\nMost striking is a cluster of micro-cap names abandoning legacy furnishings for unproven AI, energy, and minerals ventures. MWYN pivoted to AI infrastructure, ATER exited consumer brands for tantalum trading and African exploration, and XWIN repositioned as an AI platform with a SpaceX-linked stake. These transformations carry high execution risk and little track record, making them the batch’s most speculative narratives.",
